<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">83 or 84 CE · Mons Graupius, Caledonia</p><h1 class="sp-title">Calgacus to the Caledonians</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calgacus">Calgacus</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Pushing Roman power into the far north of Britain, the governor Gnaeus Julius Agricola met the gathered Caledonian peoples at Mons Graupius, a battlefield never securely identified. Tacitus, Agricola's son-in-law, gives the Caledonian leader Calgacus a speech before the battle: an indictment of Roman conquest, written by a Roman.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>To robbery, slaughter, plunder, they give the lying name of empire; they make a solitude and call it peace.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Tacitus, Agricola, 30, tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb (1876). Calgacus is known only from this work, and the speech is Tacitus' own composition.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Agricola#30" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource, tr. Church and Brodribb</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The legions won the battle, but Agricola was recalled soon afterwards and Rome never conquered the far north. Within forty years the frontier had been fixed far to the south at Hadrian's Wall. Calgacus' words became one of the most quoted judgements on empire ever written.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-graupius">Battle of Mons Graupius</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a></div></div></section>
